    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Queuing_Staffing\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_stations = 4\n    n_staff_types = 17\n    arrival_rates = [18.0, 15.0, 38.0, 14.0]  # customers/hour\n    handling_times = [[8.1, 8.8, 4.2, 11.5], [9.4, 10.7, 0.0, 5.8], [0.0, 9.1, 4.8, 3.1], [6.9, 7.5, 7.4, 4.5], [3.9, 2.1, 5.0, 5.5], [5.3, 0.0, 2.9, 1.8], [4.0, 3.7, 5.5, 0.0], [6.0, 4.1, 5.5, 1.9], [3.8, 2.9, 3.8, 2.3], [0.0, 5.9, 4.4, 3.2], [4.4, 3.1, 1.2, 5.5], [3.6, 2.4, 5.2, 4.0], [0.0, 4.7, 2.5, 1.3], [3.7, 0.0, 5.5, 5.5], [5.6, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0], [2.8, 3.1, 2.3, 3.5], [4.2, 3.2, 1.8, 4.3]]  # minutes/customer [staff_type][station]\n    hourly_costs = [15.58, 19.07, 21.64, 23.39, 25.19, 25.77, 26.45, 27.15, 28.91, 29.12, 31.68, 33.09, 36.08, 37.12, 37.98, 38.77, 41.46]  # $/hour per staff type\n    available_staff = [2.0, 3.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.0, 2.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.0, 3.0, 2.0, 3.0, 2.0, 3.0, 3.0, 3.0, 3.0]  # max available per type\n    max_utilization = [0.84, 0.86, 0.83, 0.89]  # utilization cap per station\n    min_staff = [2.0, 1.0, 2.0, 2.0]  # minimum staff per station\n\n    # COMPUTE service rates from handling times (INDUCED)\n    # handling_time is in minutes/customer, we need customers/hour\n    service_rate = {}\n    for k in range(n_staff_types):\n        for i in range(n_stations):\n            ht = handling_times[k][i]\n            service_rate[(k,i)] = 60.0 / ht if ht > 0 else 0.0\n\n    # COMPUTE capacity thresholds (INDUCED)\n    min_capacity = [arrival_rates[i] / max_utilization[i] for i in range(n_stations)]\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    assign = {}\n    for k in range(n_staff_types):\n        for i in range(n_stations):\n            ub = int(available_staff[k]) if handling_times[k][i] > 0 else 0\n            assign[(k,i)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=ub, vtype=GRB.INTEGER,\n                                     name=f\"Assign_K{k}_S{i}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize total hourly cost ---\n    m.setObjective(\n        quicksum(hourly_costs[k] * assign[(k,i)]\n                 for k in range(n_staff_types) for i in range(n_stations)),\n        GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # Staff availability\n    for k in range(n_staff_types):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(assign[(k,i)] for i in range(n_stations)) <= available_staff[k],\n            name=f\"Avail_K{k}\")\n\n    # Capacity (induced): aggregate service rate >= arrival / max_util\n    for i in range(n_stations):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(service_rate[(k,i)] * assign[(k,i)]\n                     for k in range(n_staff_types) if handling_times[k][i] > 0)\n            >= min_capacity[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_S{i}\")\n\n    # Minimum staffing\n    for i in range(n_stations):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(assign[(k,i)] for k in range(n_staff_types)\n                     if handling_times[k][i] > 0)\n            >= min_staff[i],\n            name=f\"MinStaff_S{i}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Staffing review for multi-station service center \u2013 balancing service quality and cost\n\nOverview\nWe are preparing a staffing plan for our multi-station service center to align resources with expected customer demand while maintaining our service standards. The center comprises distinct service points (help desks, counters, and departments) that experience different arrival patterns. Our workforce includes several staff types with varying skills, speeds, and hourly costs. Some staff types are qualified to work at certain stations while others are not.\n\nSituation\n- Demand varies by station and is tracked as customers arriving per hour. These rates are known and stable enough for planning.\n- Work is completed at different speeds depending on who is assigned and where. Handling speed is recorded as the average minutes per customer for each staff type at each station.\n- Not all staff-station combinations are allowed; unqualified pairings are excluded from assignment.\n\nStaffing challenge\nWe must decide how many people of each staff type to place at each service station to absorb incoming demand reliably and cost-effectively. Because handling times are stated in minutes while arrivals are tracked hourly, we will account for the difference in units when comparing expected workload to the team\u2019s processing capability. Practically, this means the combined capacity of the assigned team at each station should comfortably outpace the expected arrivals so that staff are not operating at the ceiling. At the same time, we need to respect limits on how many people of each staff type are available and avoid assigning anyone to a station they are not qualified to serve.\n\nService standards and operating policies\n- Staff workload: People should not be busy more than the target fraction of their time. We will staff each station so there is headroom between expected arrivals and what the team can process, preserving stability during normal variation.\n- Minimum coverage: Each station requires a defined baseline number of on-duty staff for presence, safety, and customer navigation even at lower volumes.\n- Qualifications: Only qualified staff types may be assigned to each station; restricted pairings are not considered.\n- Availability: The total number of people of each staff type is limited and cannot be exceeded.\n- Cost discipline: Hourly labor costs vary by staff type. Where qualifications allow, we will evaluate trade-offs between faster, higher-cost resources and slower, lower-cost alternatives to meet demand at the lowest feasible hourly cost.\n\nGoal and outputs\nOur goal is to assign staff to stations in a way that meets all service requirements at minimum total hourly cost. The output will be a station-by-station staffing plan that:\n- Specifies the number of people of each staff type to assign to each station (qualified pairings only)\n- Ensures each station\u2019s team capacity comfortably exceeds expected arrivals within the target busy-time level\n- Meets minimum coverage at each station\n- Respects the total availability of each staff type\n- Reports total hourly labor cost and highlights any binding constraints (e.g., tight availability or qualifications)\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm the arrival profiles, handling times by staff type and station, hourly costs, staff availability, utilization target, and minimum coverage.\n- Generate a proposed assignment plan and review with station managers for practicality (breaks, cross-coverage, training).\n- Validate sensitivity to demand shifts and policy changes, and finalize the plan for the scheduling cycle.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Operational Data\n- Number of service stations: 4\n- Number of staff types: 17\n- Arrival rates by station (customers per hour): Arrival Rates (customers per hour):\n  - Station 0: 18 customers/hour\n  - Station 1: 15 customers/hour\n  - Station 2: 38 customers/hour\n  - Station 3: 14 customers/hour\n- Average handling times by staff type and station (minutes per customer): Average Handling Time (minutes per customer, by staff type and station):\n  A value of 0 means the staff type is not qualified for that station.\nStaff Type \\ Station | S0 | S1 | S2 | S3\n----------------------------------------\nType 0              |   8.1 |   8.8 |   4.2 |  11.5\nType 1              |   9.4 |  10.7 |   N/A |   5.8\nType 2              |   N/A |   9.1 |   4.8 |   3.1\nType 3              |   6.9 |   7.5 |   7.4 |   4.5\nType 4              |   3.9 |   2.1 |   5.0 |   5.5\nType 5              |   5.3 |   N/A |   2.9 |   1.8\nType 6              |   4.0 |   3.7 |   5.5 |   N/A\nType 7              |   6.0 |   4.1 |   5.5 |   1.9\nType 8              |   3.8 |   2.9 |   3.8 |   2.3\nType 9              |   N/A |   5.9 |   4.4 |   3.2\nType 10              |   4.4 |   3.1 |   1.2 |   5.5\nType 11              |   3.6 |   2.4 |   5.2 |   4.0\nType 12              |   N/A |   4.7 |   2.5 |   1.3\nType 13              |   3.7 |   N/A |   5.5 |   5.5\nType 14              |   5.6 |   N/A |   N/A |   N/A\nType 15              |   2.8 |   3.1 |   2.3 |   3.5\nType 16              |   4.2 |   3.2 |   1.8 |   4.3\n- Hourly cost per staff type: [15.58, 19.07, 21.64, 23.39, 25.19, 25.77, 26.45, 27.15, 28.91, 29.12, 31.68, 33.09, 36.08, 37.12, 37.98, 38.77, 41.46]\n- Available staff by type: [2.0, 3.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.0, 2.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.0, 3.0, 2.0, 3.0, 2.0, 3.0, 3.0, 3.0, 3.0]\n- Target maximum utilization (fraction of time busy): Maximum Utilization Policy (fraction of time staff may be busy):\n  - Station 0: 0.84\n  - Station 1: 0.86\n  - Station 2: 0.83\n  - Station 3: 0.89\n- Minimum staffing requirement by station: [2.0, 1.0, 2.0, 2.0]",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Staffing review for multi-station service center \u2013 balancing service quality and cost\n\nOverview\nWe are preparing a staffing plan for our multi-station service center to align resources with expected customer demand while maintaining our service standards. The center comprises distinct service points (help desks, counters, and departments) that experience different arrival patterns. Our workforce includes several staff types with varying skills, speeds, and hourly costs. Some staff types are qualified to work at certain stations while others are not.\n\nSituation\n- Demand varies by station and is tracked as customers arriving per hour. These rates are known and stable enough for planning.\n- Work is completed at different speeds depending on who is assigned and where. Handling speed is recorded as the average minutes per customer for each staff type at each station.\n- Not all staff-station combinations are allowed; unqualified pairings are excluded from assignment.\n\nStaffing challenge\nWe must decide how many people of each staff type to place at each service station to absorb incoming demand reliably and cost-effectively. Because handling times are stated in minutes while arrivals are tracked hourly, we will account for the difference in units when comparing expected workload to the team\u2019s processing capability. Practically, this means the combined capacity of the assigned team at each station should comfortably outpace the expected arrivals so that staff are not operating at the ceiling. At the same time, we need to respect limits on how many people of each staff type are available and avoid assigning anyone to a station they are not qualified to serve.\n\nService standards and operating policies\n- Staff workload: People should not be busy more than the target fraction of their time. We will staff each station so there is headroom between expected arrivals and what the team can process, preserving stability during normal variation.\n- Minimum coverage: Each station requires a defined baseline number of on-duty staff for presence, safety, and customer navigation even at lower volumes.\n- Qualifications: Only qualified staff types may be assigned to each station; restricted pairings are not considered.\n- Availability: The total number of people of each staff type is limited and cannot be exceeded.\n- Cost discipline: Hourly labor costs vary by staff type. Where qualifications allow, we will evaluate trade-offs between faster, higher-cost resources and slower, lower-cost alternatives to meet demand at the lowest feasible hourly cost.\n\nGoal and outputs\nOur goal is to assign staff to stations in a way that meets all service requirements at minimum total hourly cost. The output will be a station-by-station staffing plan that:\n- Specifies the number of people of each staff type to assign to each station (qualified pairings only)\n- Ensures each station\u2019s team capacity comfortably exceeds expected arrivals within the target busy-time level\n- Meets minimum coverage at each station\n- Respects the total availability of each staff type\n- Reports total hourly labor cost and highlights any binding constraints (e.g., tight availability or qualifications)\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm the arrival profiles, handling times by staff type and station, hourly costs, staff availability, utilization target, and minimum coverage.\n- Generate a proposed assignment plan and review with station managers for practicality (breaks, cross-coverage, training).\n- Validate sensitivity to demand shifts and policy changes, and finalize the plan for the scheduling cycle.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Operational Data\n- Number of service stations: {NUM_STATIONS}\n- Number of staff types: {NUM_STAFF_TYPES}\n- Arrival rates by station (customers per hour): {ARRIVAL_RATES}\n- Average handling times by staff type and station (minutes per customer): {HANDLING_TIMES}\n- Hourly cost per staff type: {HOURLY_COSTS}\n- Available staff by type: {AVAILABLE_STAFF}\n- Target maximum utilization (fraction of time busy): {MAX_UTILIZATION}\n- Minimum staffing requirement by station: {MIN_STAFF}"
